ynamics. These metrics help clinicians evaluate individual responsiveness and adjust therapy accordingly [Data: Relationships (2611, 2615, 2619, 2621, 2624); Entities (13016, 13142, 13602, 13923, 13938)]. In cases of suspected interaction (e.g., with fluconazole), thromboelastography is specifically recommended for monitoring [Data: Relationships (2624)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Clopidogrel interacts with other antiplatelet agents and drug classes, posing complex management challenges\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Clopidogrel is frequently co-administered with other antiplatelet drugs, creating potential for additive effects or pharmacodynamic interactions. Additionally, it interacts with dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and proton pump inhibitors through shared metabolic pathways (e.g., CYP3A4, CYP2C19). Although some combinations (e.g., with pantoprazole or rabeprazole) show minimal impact, others (e.g., with omeprazole) significantly impair clopidogrel activation [Data: Relationships (41605, 30276); Entities (11022, 10214)]. These interactions necessitate careful review of concomitant medications, particularly in elderly patients with polypharmacy [Data: Relationships (22208)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Cardiovascular events remain a key adverse outcome linked to suboptimal antiplatelet therapy\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Despite guideline-directed use, inadequate platelet inhibition—due to genetic factors, drug interactions, or non-adherence—can lead to breakthrough cardiovascular events. While clopidogrel itself is used to prevent such events, its compromised efficacy (e.g., in CYP2C19 poor metabolizers or when co-administered with strong inhibitors) may paradoxically contribute to this risk. Other drugs like ritonavir are independently associated with increased cardiovascular event risk, highlighting the broader context of medication-related cardiovascular safety [Data: Relationships (